a) To change glutamic acid (Glu) to lysine (Lys), three base substitutions are needed because lysine is a larger amino acid than glutamic acid.

b) To change leucine (Leu) to alanine (Ala), one base substitution is needed because alanine is a smaller amino acid than leucine.

c) To change phenylalanine (Phe) to glycine (Gly), one base substitution is needed because glycine is a smaller amino acid than phenylalanine.

d) To change tryptophan (Trp) to serine (Ser), one base substitution is needed because serine is a smaller amino acid than tryptophan.

e) To change valine (Val) to glutamic acid (Glu), three base substitutions are needed because glutamic acid is a larger amino acid than valine.

Therefore, the correct answer is option B. 2 for glutamic acid to lysine, 3 for leucine to alanine, 1 for phenylalanine to glycine, 1 for tryptophan to serine, 3 for valine to glutamic acid.
